SETSID(2)                      P32W Unix                       SETSID(2)

NAME
       setsid - give up the controlling terminal

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       pid_t setsid(void);

DESCRIPTION
       setsid detaches the calling process from its controlling
       terminal. The next terminal it opens (/dev/tty0 or
       /dev/tty1) becomes its new controlling terminal, the one
       whose ^C and ^\ signal it and that /dev/tty refers to.
       init(8) calls it in each child before it runs getty(8), so
       that the terminal getty opens becomes that child's; cron(8)
       calls it to run without one.

RETURN VALUE
       The caller's process ID. The call cannot fail.

SEE ALSO
       open(2), ioctl(2), init(8), getty(8), cron(8)

BUGS
       Despite the name there are no sessions or process groups:
       setsid only clears the controlling terminal. It succeeds
       even for a process that would be a group leader in POSIX.
